(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("infra-design",{"infra-design/designing-for-operators-requirements-day-two-and-the-design-review":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure design differs from product design. The users are engineers, the service is depended on by many teams, and the cost of a bad design is paid during upgrades and outages for years.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requirements from internal customers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Back of the envelope sizing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes and blast radius for shared services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Day two: upgrades, migrations and ownership\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The design review and decision record\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requirements\">Requirements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List who uses the service, what they do with it and what they expect. Ask for numbers: how many builds a day, how many nodes, how large the data and how fast it must recover. Separate must have from nice to have, and write down what is out of scope. Requirements from users who are not in the room will otherwise be discovered after launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sizing\">Sizing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Estimate load, storage and growth with simple arithmetic and state the assumptions. For example, two thousand builds a day at ten minutes each and a peak of five times the average gives the runner capacity. Check the result against a measured pilot. The aim is the right order of magnitude, not false precision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius\">Blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A shared service fails for all its users together. Decide how to limit that: separate instances per environment, rings for changes, quotas per tenant so that one cannot take all capacity, and a degraded mode that keeps the critical path working. Write the dependencies, including the ones that the service needs to start itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"day-two\">Day two\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plan upgrades, migrations, backups, capacity growth and on call from the start. Ask how a new version is rolled out and rolled back, how a tenant is moved and how the service is rebuilt from nothing. A design that cannot be upgraded safely will be left on old versions until it fails. Decide the owner and the support model before building.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review\">Review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a short design document with goals, non goals, the proposed design, alternatives and risks, then review it with operators and users. Use a checklist: what fails, how it is detected, how it recovers, what it costs and what is the smallest first version. Record the decision and the reasons in a decision record.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"req&gt;size;size&gt;fail;fail&gt;day2\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Designing for operators: requirements, day two and the design review\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Requirements\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"size\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sizing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fail\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Failure modes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"day2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Day two plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Requirements to Sizing; 2, Sizing to Failure modes; 3, Failure modes to Day two plan.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Requirements \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Users and numbers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"size\">\u003cdt>Sizing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Order of magnitude.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Guess.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fail\">\u003cdt>Failure modes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Blast radius.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared fate.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"day2\">\u003cdt>Day two plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Upgrade and own.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Orphaned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-registry-that-nobody-could-upgrade\">Incident: The registry that nobody could upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A shared registry stayed on a three year old version because upgrading risked every pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath=&quot;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&quot;\nkubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath=&quot;{.spec.replicas}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/registry:2.7.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.spec.replicas}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service was built with one replica, no staging copy and no rollback plan, so every team feared an upgrade. A security fix finally forced it, and the upgrade caused two hours of failed builds. The replacement design had two replicas behind a balancer, a staging instance, a documented rollback and a quarterly upgrade rehearsal, and it was reviewed with its users before launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No requirements from internal users\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing with no stated assumptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One shared instance with no isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No plan for upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No named owner\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"sizing","text":"Sizing"},{"id":"blast-radius","text":"Blast radius"},{"id":"day-two","text":"Day two"},{"id":"review","text":"Review"},{"id":"incident-the-registry-that-nobody-could-upgrade","text":"Incident: The registry that nobody could upgrade"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: design and operations","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/introduction/"},{"t":"Distributed system design patterns","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/"},{"t":"Kubernetes node upgrade practices","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/"}],"text":"Infrastructure design differs from product design. The users are engineers, the service is depended on by many teams, and the cost of a bad design is paid during upgrades and outages for years. What you will learn Requirements from internal customers Back of the envelope sizing Failure modes and blast radius for shared services Day two: upgrades, migrations and ownership The design review and decision record Requirements List who uses the service, what they do with it and what they expect. Ask for numbers: how many builds a day, how many nodes, how large the data and how fast it must recover. Separate must have from nice to have, and write down what is out of scope. Requirements from users who are not in the room will otherwise be discovered after launch. Sizing Estimate load, storage and growth with simple arithmetic and state the assumptions. For example, two thousand builds a day at ten minutes each and a peak of five times the average gives the runner capacity. Check the result against a measured pilot. The aim is the right order of magnitude, not false precision. Blast radius A shared service fails for all its users together. Decide how to limit that: separate instances per environment, rings for changes, quotas per tenant so that one cannot take all capacity, and a degraded mode that keeps the critical path working. Write the dependencies, including the ones that the service needs to start itself. Day two Plan upgrades, migrations, backups, capacity growth and on call from the start. Ask how a new version is rolled out and rolled back, how a tenant is moved and how the service is rebuilt from nothing. A design that cannot be upgraded safely will be left on old versions until it fails. Decide the owner and the support model before building. Review Write a short design document with goals, non goals, the proposed design, alternatives and risks, then review it with operators and users. Use a checklist: what fails, how it is detected, how it recovers, what it costs and what is the smallest first version. Record the decision and the reasons in a decision record. Designing for operators: requirements, day two and the design review Flow in order: 1, Requirements to Sizing; 2, Sizing to Failure modes; 3, Failure modes to Day two plan. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Requirements Step What it does: Users and numbers. If it fails or is weak: Missing users. Sizing Step What it does: Order of magnitude. If it fails or is weak: Guess. Failure modes Security What it does: Blast radius. If it fails or is weak: Shared fate. Day two plan Step What it does: Upgrade and own. If it fails or is weak: Orphaned. Incident: The registry that nobody could upgrade Incident A shared registry stayed on a three year old version because upgrading risked every pipeline. $ kubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath= \"{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}\" registry.example.com/registry:2.7.1 $ kubectl get deploy registry -n infra -o jsonpath= \"{.spec.replicas}\" 1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The service was built with one replica, no staging copy and no rollback plan, so every team feared an upgrade. A security fix finally forced it, and the upgrade caused two hours of failed builds. The replacement design had two replicas behind a balancer, a staging instance, a documented rollback and a quarterly upgrade rehearsal, and it was reviewed with its users before launch. Common mistakes No requirements from internal users Sizing with no stated assumptions One shared instance with no isolation No plan for upgrades No named owner"},"infra-design/design-a-ci-build-platform-queues-runners-caches-and-isolation":{"html":"\u003cp>Developers wait on the build platform many times a day. It must be fast, fair and safe, because it runs untrusted code with access to secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requirements and sizing a build farm\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Queue, scheduler and autoscaled runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caches: dependencies, layers and remote build cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolation and secrets for untrusted code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes: queue backlog, cache poisoning and runaway jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requirements\">Requirements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assume three thousand builds a day with a peak of ten times the average at the start of working hours. Goals might be that eighty percent of jobs start within one minute and that a failed runner never loses queued work. Non goals might include long running service hosting. Measure queue time and build time separately, because they have different fixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"queue-and-runners\">Queue and runners\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A job queue holds pending work, and a scheduler assigns it to runners with the right labels. Runners are created on demand, in containers or short lived virtual machines, and removed after use. Keep a warm pool for the morning peak, scale on queue length and set maximum limits so that a loop cannot create thousands of runners.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caches\">Caches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependency caches avoid downloading the same packages. Layer caches speed up image builds. A remote build cache shares outputs between jobs and developers. Key caches by content and tool version, keep them near the runners to avoid transfer cost and set a retention policy so that they do not grow without limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"isolation-and-secrets\">Isolation and secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assume that a job can be malicious, because it runs code from pull requests. Use a fresh runner for each job, no shared host directories, restricted network egress and no access to production. Provide short lived credentials through federation and only to protected branches. Never allow forks to read secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-modes\">Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A backlog appears when runners cannot start, so alert on queue wait time. A poisoned cache lets one job affect another, so make caches read only for untrusted jobs. A runaway job can fill disks or run for days, so set time and resource limits. Plan for the failure of the platform itself with a documented emergency path to release.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"q&gt;sch;sch&gt;run;run&gt;cache\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Design: a CI build platform with queues, runners, caches and isolation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Job queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sch\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ephemeral runners\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cache and artifacts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Job queue to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Ephemeral runners; 3, Ephemeral runners to Cache and artifacts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Job queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pending work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Backlog.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sch\">\u003cdt>Scheduler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Labels and quotas.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Starved.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Ephemeral runners \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One job each.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reused.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Cache and artifacts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keyed by content.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Poisoned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-builds-waited-forty-minutes-at-nine-in-the-morning\">Incident: Builds waited forty minutes at nine in the morning\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Developers reported that every build was queued for most of the morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n runners --no-headers | awk '{print $3}' | sort | uniq -c\nkubectl describe nodes | grep -c &quot;insufficient&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n runners --no-headers | awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $3}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 12 Running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 240 Pending\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe nodes | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;insufficient&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The autoscaler had a maximum of twelve runner nodes, set when the team was small, and two hundred and forty jobs waited for capacity. The design review added a warm pool for the morning, a scale up rule on queue wait time, raised the limit with a cost cap and a fairness quota per team so that one repository could not take all runners. Queue time fell to under one minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reusing runners between jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving fork builds access to secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A scaling limit nobody reviews\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No quotas per team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared caches writable by untrusted jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"queue-and-runners","text":"Queue and runners"},{"id":"caches","text":"Caches"},{"id":"isolation-and-secrets","text":"Isolation and secrets"},{"id":"failure-modes","text":"Failure modes"},{"id":"incident-builds-waited-forty-minutes-at-nine-in-the-morning","text":"Incident: Builds waited forty minutes at nine in the morning"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes cluster autoscaler","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler"},{"t":"GitHub Actions runner scale sets","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/hosting-your-own-runners/managing-self-hosted-runners-with-actions-runner-controller/about-actions-runner-controller"},{"t":"Remote APIs for build caching","u":"https://github.com/bazelbuild/remote-apis"}],"text":"Developers wait on the build platform many times a day. It must be fast, fair and safe, because it runs untrusted code with access to secrets. What you will learn Requirements and sizing a build farm Queue, scheduler and autoscaled runners Caches: dependencies, layers and remote build cache Isolation and secrets for untrusted code Failure modes: queue backlog, cache poisoning and runaway jobs Requirements Assume three thousand builds a day with a peak of ten times the average at the start of working hours. Goals might be that eighty percent of jobs start within one minute and that a failed runner never loses queued work. Non goals might include long running service hosting. Measure queue time and build time separately, because they have different fixes. Queue and runners A job queue holds pending work, and a scheduler assigns it to runners with the right labels. Runners are created on demand, in containers or short lived virtual machines, and removed after use. Keep a warm pool for the morning peak, scale on queue length and set maximum limits so that a loop cannot create thousands of runners. Caches Dependency caches avoid downloading the same packages. Layer caches speed up image builds. A remote build cache shares outputs between jobs and developers. Key caches by content and tool version, keep them near the runners to avoid transfer cost and set a retention policy so that they do not grow without limit. Isolation and secrets Assume that a job can be malicious, because it runs code from pull requests. Use a fresh runner for each job, no shared host directories, restricted network egress and no access to production. Provide short lived credentials through federation and only to protected branches. Never allow forks to read secrets. Failure modes A backlog appears when runners cannot start, so alert on queue wait time. A poisoned cache lets one job affect another, so make caches read only for untrusted jobs. A runaway job can fill disks or run for days, so set time and resource limits. Plan for the failure of the platform itself with a documented emergency path to release. Design: a CI build platform with queues, runners, caches and isolation Flow in order: 1, Job queue to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Ephemeral runners; 3, Ephemeral runners to Cache and artifacts. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Job queue Data store What it does: Pending work. If it fails or is weak: Backlog. Scheduler Step What it does: Labels and quotas. If it fails or is weak: Starved. Ephemeral runners Service What it does: One job each. If it fails or is weak: Reused. Cache and artifacts Data store What it does: Keyed by content. If it fails or is weak: Poisoned. Incident: Builds waited forty minutes at nine in the morning Incident Developers reported that every build was queued for most of the morning. $ kubectl get pods -n runners --no-headers | awk &#x27;{print $3}&#x27; | sort | uniq -c 12 Running 240 Pending $ kubectl describe nodes | grep -c \"insufficient\" 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The autoscaler had a maximum of twelve runner nodes, set when the team was small, and two hundred and forty jobs waited for capacity. The design review added a warm pool for the morning, a scale up rule on queue wait time, raised the limit with a cost cap and a fairness quota per team so that one repository could not take all runners. Queue time fell to under one minute. Common mistakes Reusing runners between jobs Giving fork builds access to secrets A scaling limit nobody reviews No quotas per team Shared caches writable by untrusted jobs"},"infra-design/design-a-registry-and-artifact-distribution-service":{"html":"\u003cp>Every deployment pulls from a registry. If it is slow or down, you cannot scale, replace failed nodes or roll back, so it deserves the same care as a database.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requirements and sizing: storage, pulls and bandwidth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Architecture: object storage, metadata and a content cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication and pull through caches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Immutability, signing and retention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes: outage during scale up, rate limits and storage growth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requirements\">Requirements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Estimate the number of images and their size, pushes per day and, more importantly, pulls. A cluster of five hundred nodes pulling a one gigabyte image at once is half a terabyte of traffic. State goals such as availability for pulls higher than for pushes, and a pull time target. Existing images must remain available for rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"architecture\">Architecture\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store layers in durable object storage and metadata in a database, behind stateless registry servers with a balancer. Layers are content addressed, so they can be cached safely and shared between images. Separate the read path from the write path so that a push storm does not slow pulls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-and-caches\">Replication and caches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Replicate to each region or cluster so that pulls stay local and survive the loss of a region. A pull through cache stores images from external registries, which protects against upstream outages and rate limits. Warm caches or pre pull large images to nodes before a big rollout, and use a peer to peer layer sharing option for very large fleets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"immutability-and-retention\">Immutability and retention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make tags immutable in production repositories, deploy by digest and sign images at build. Set retention rules to remove untagged and old images but never the ones referenced by running workloads or recent releases. Check what is in use before running a clean up, and run the clean up with a dry run first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-modes\">Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the registry is down during a scale up, new nodes cannot start pods. Keep a node level cache and a second read replica. External rate limits can block a cluster, so pull through and authenticate. Storage grows steadily, so track it per team and charge back. Test restoring the registry from backups and the loss of one region.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"push&gt;reg;reg&gt;obj;obj&gt;pull\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Design: a registry and artifact distribution service\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"push\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Push path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Registry servers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obj\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pull\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull caches\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Push path to Registry servers; 2, Registry servers to Object storage; 3, Object storage to Pull caches.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"push\">\u003cdt>Push path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CI builds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Storm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Registry servers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stateless.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obj\">\u003cdt>Object storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Content addressed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Growth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pull\">\u003cdt>Pull caches \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Regional.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cold.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-scale-up-that-could-not-pull-images\">Incident: A scale up that could not pull images\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a traffic peak, new pods stayed in image pull for ten minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector reason=Failed | tail -2\nkubectl get pods -n shop --no-headers | grep -c ImagePullBackOff\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector reason=Failed | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Failed to pull image: toomanyrequests: You have reached your pull rate limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop --no-headers | grep -c ImagePullBackOff\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">38\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The deployment used a base image from a public registry that limited anonymous pulls per address, and all nodes shared a few outgoing addresses, so the limit was reached at the worst time. The platform team added an authenticated pull through cache in each cluster, mirrored the critical base images into the internal registry and added an alert on pull failures. The next scale up pulled from the local cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying on a public registry in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutable tags for production images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Clean up that removes images still in use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One region with no replica\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No pre pull before large rollouts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"architecture","text":"Architecture"},{"id":"replication-and-caches","text":"Replication and caches"},{"id":"immutability-and-retention","text":"Immutability and retention"},{"id":"failure-modes","text":"Failure modes"},{"id":"incident-a-scale-up-that-could-not-pull-images","text":"Incident: A scale up that could not pull images"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Distribution registry","u":"https://github.com/distribution/distribution"},{"t":"OCI distribution specification","u":"https://github.com/opencontainers/distribution-spec"},{"t":"Docker Hub download rate limit","u":"https://docs.docker.com/docker-hub/usage/"}],"text":"Every deployment pulls from a registry. If it is slow or down, you cannot scale, replace failed nodes or roll back, so it deserves the same care as a database. What you will learn Requirements and sizing: storage, pulls and bandwidth Architecture: object storage, metadata and a content cache Replication and pull through caches Immutability, signing and retention Failure modes: outage during scale up, rate limits and storage growth Requirements Estimate the number of images and their size, pushes per day and, more importantly, pulls. A cluster of five hundred nodes pulling a one gigabyte image at once is half a terabyte of traffic. State goals such as availability for pulls higher than for pushes, and a pull time target. Existing images must remain available for rollback. Architecture Store layers in durable object storage and metadata in a database, behind stateless registry servers with a balancer. Layers are content addressed, so they can be cached safely and shared between images. Separate the read path from the write path so that a push storm does not slow pulls. Replication and caches Replicate to each region or cluster so that pulls stay local and survive the loss of a region. A pull through cache stores images from external registries, which protects against upstream outages and rate limits. Warm caches or pre pull large images to nodes before a big rollout, and use a peer to peer layer sharing option for very large fleets. Immutability and retention Make tags immutable in production repositories, deploy by digest and sign images at build. Set retention rules to remove untagged and old images but never the ones referenced by running workloads or recent releases. Check what is in use before running a clean up, and run the clean up with a dry run first. Failure modes If the registry is down during a scale up, new nodes cannot start pods. Keep a node level cache and a second read replica. External rate limits can block a cluster, so pull through and authenticate. Storage grows steadily, so track it per team and charge back. Test restoring the registry from backups and the loss of one region. Design: a registry and artifact distribution service Flow in order: 1, Push path to Registry servers; 2, Registry servers to Object storage; 3, Object storage to Pull caches. Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Push path Service What it does: CI builds. If it fails or is weak: Storm. Registry servers Service What it does: Stateless. If it fails or is weak: Down. Object storage Data store What it does: Content addressed. If it fails or is weak: Growth. Pull caches Service What it does: Regional. If it fails or is weak: Cold. Incident: A scale up that could not pull images Incident During a traffic peak, new pods stayed in image pull for ten minutes. $ kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector reason=Failed | tail -2 Failed to pull image: toomanyrequests: You have reached your pull rate limit $ kubectl get pods -n shop --no-headers | grep -c ImagePullBackOff 38 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The deployment used a base image from a public registry that limited anonymous pulls per address, and all nodes shared a few outgoing addresses, so the limit was reached at the worst time. The platform team added an authenticated pull through cache in each cluster, mirrored the critical base images into the internal registry and added an alert on pull failures. The next scale up pulled from the local cache. Common mistakes Relying on a public registry in production Mutable tags for production images Clean up that removes images still in use One region with no replica No pre pull before large rollouts"},"infra-design/design-fleet-provisioning-and-patching-at-scale":{"html":"\u003cp>A fleet that cannot be patched safely is a fleet that will be breached or broken. The design has to make the safe way the easy way.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requirements: fleet size, change windows and compliance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Image pipeline versus configuration management\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rings and health gates for rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handling drift and stragglers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes: bad image, stuck rollout and unpatchable hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requirements\">Requirements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Estimate the number of hosts, their roles and how fast a critical patch must be applied, for example ninety five percent within seven days. Note which hosts hold state and need care, which can be replaced freely and where network or bandwidth limits affect rollout speed. Compliance often sets the deadlines, so ask for them early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"images-or-configuration\">Images or configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An image pipeline builds a tested machine image on a schedule, and hosts are replaced with the new image. This gives repeatable hosts and simple rollback, and suits stateless roles. Configuration management changes hosts in place, which suits stateful servers and bare metal. Many fleets use both, with a clear rule for which role uses which.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rings-and-gates\">Rings and gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Roll out in rings: a few test hosts, a canary group, then growing shares of the fleet with a pause between rings. Gate each step on health signals such as error rate, boot success and the number of hosts reporting. Stop automatically on failure and keep the previous image available. Avoid patching all replicas of a service in the same window.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift-and-stragglers\">Drift and stragglers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some hosts will be offline, locked or failing. Track the version of every host in an inventory and report the share on the current version by ring and by owner. Chase stragglers with clear owners and a deadline, and after the deadline isolate or replace them. A dashboard of patch age by team creates a healthy pressure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-modes\">Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A bad image that fails to boot can stop a whole ring, so test boot and a service check before release and keep automatic rollback. A rollout can stall if a drain never completes, so set timeouts. Hosts that cannot be patched, such as old appliances, need a documented exception with compensating controls and an end date.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"img&gt;ring;ring&gt;gate;gate&gt;inv\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Design: fleet provisioning and patching at scale\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"img\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Image pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ring\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollout rings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gate\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Health gates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inventory and stragglers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Image pipeline to Rollout rings; 2, Rollout rings to Health gates; 3, Health gates to Inventory and stragglers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"img\">\u003cdt>Image pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tested build.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad image.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ring\">\u003cdt>Rollout rings \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Canary first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stalled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gate\">\u003cdt>Health gates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stop on failure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Inventory and stragglers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Version per host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unknown.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-kernel-update-that-did-not-boot\">Incident: The kernel update that did not boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A patch rollout left fifty hosts down after a reboot because a network driver was missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"uname -r\njournalctl -b -1 -p err --no-pager | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uname\u003c/span> -r\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -b -1 -p err --no-pager | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dracut: FATAL: no network interface found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rollout had no canary ring and no boot check, so the first ring was fifty hosts of one hardware type and all of them failed to bring up the network. The team rolled back to the previous image from the boot menu on the management network. The redesign added a hardware diverse canary ring, a post boot health gate and an automatic stop, and the next kernel update stopped after two hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No canary ring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patching all replicas of a service together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No health gate after reboot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No inventory of versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open ended exceptions for unpatched hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"images-or-configuration","text":"Images or configuration"},{"id":"rings-and-gates","text":"Rings and gates"},{"id":"drift-and-stragglers","text":"Drift and stragglers"},{"id":"failure-modes","text":"Failure modes"},{"id":"incident-the-kernel-update-that-did-not-boot","text":"Incident: The kernel update that did not boot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Safe system updates for fleets","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"},{"t":"Kubernetes node upgrade practices","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/"},{"t":"Image pull through cache","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/pull-through-cache.html"}],"text":"A fleet that cannot be patched safely is a fleet that will be breached or broken. The design has to make the safe way the easy way. What you will learn Requirements: fleet size, change windows and compliance Image pipeline versus configuration management Rings and health gates for rollout Handling drift and stragglers Failure modes: bad image, stuck rollout and unpatchable hosts Requirements Estimate the number of hosts, their roles and how fast a critical patch must be applied, for example ninety five percent within seven days. Note which hosts hold state and need care, which can be replaced freely and where network or bandwidth limits affect rollout speed. Compliance often sets the deadlines, so ask for them early. Images or configuration An image pipeline builds a tested machine image on a schedule, and hosts are replaced with the new image. This gives repeatable hosts and simple rollback, and suits stateless roles. Configuration management changes hosts in place, which suits stateful servers and bare metal. Many fleets use both, with a clear rule for which role uses which. Rings and gates Roll out in rings: a few test hosts, a canary group, then growing shares of the fleet with a pause between rings. Gate each step on health signals such as error rate, boot success and the number of hosts reporting. Stop automatically on failure and keep the previous image available. Avoid patching all replicas of a service in the same window. Drift and stragglers Some hosts will be offline, locked or failing. Track the version of every host in an inventory and report the share on the current version by ring and by owner. Chase stragglers with clear owners and a deadline, and after the deadline isolate or replace them. A dashboard of patch age by team creates a healthy pressure. Failure modes A bad image that fails to boot can stop a whole ring, so test boot and a service check before release and keep automatic rollback. A rollout can stall if a drain never completes, so set timeouts. Hosts that cannot be patched, such as old appliances, need a documented exception with compensating controls and an end date. Design: fleet provisioning and patching at scale Flow in order: 1, Image pipeline to Rollout rings; 2, Rollout rings to Health gates; 3, Health gates to Inventory and stragglers. Step Observability Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Image pipeline Step What it does: Tested build. If it fails or is weak: Bad image. Rollout rings Step What it does: Canary first. If it fails or is weak: Stalled. Health gates Observability What it does: Stop on failure. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Inventory and stragglers Data store What it does: Version per host. If it fails or is weak: Unknown. Incident: The kernel update that did not boot Incident A patch rollout left fifty hosts down after a reboot because a network driver was missing. $ uname -r 5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64 $ journalctl -b -1 -p err --no-pager | tail -2 dracut: FATAL: no network interface found (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The rollout had no canary ring and no boot check, so the first ring was fifty hosts of one hardware type and all of them failed to bring up the network. The team rolled back to the previous image from the boot menu on the management network. The redesign added a hardware diverse canary ring, a post boot health gate and an automatic stop, and the next kernel update stopped after two hosts. Common mistakes No canary ring Patching all replicas of a service together No health gate after reboot No inventory of versions Open ended exceptions for unpatched hosts"},"infra-design/design-an-ingress-and-load-balancing-tier":{"html":"\u003cp>Every request passes through the front door. It terminates TLS, spreads load and shields backends, and when it fails everything behind it looks down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requirements: traffic, latency, protocols and availability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Layer 4 or layer 7 and where to terminate TLS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks, draining and connection limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Zones, regions and failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes: overload, bad configuration and certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requirements\">Requirements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Estimate requests per second at peak, concurrent connections, bandwidth and the protocols in use, such as HTTP, gRPC and WebSocket. Set an availability goal for the tier, which must be higher than for the services behind it. Ask whether clients are external, internal or both, since that changes the security design.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layer-4-and-layer-7\">Layer 4 and layer 7\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A layer 4 balancer forwards connections and is fast and simple. A layer 7 proxy understands HTTP, so it can route by host and path, retry, limit rates and add headers, at a higher cost per request. Many designs use a layer 4 tier in front of a layer 7 tier. Terminate TLS at the layer 7 tier, and re encrypt to the backends if the network is not trusted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-and-limits\">Health and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Health checks should test that the backend can do useful work, not only that the port is open, but they must be cheap and must not take down a struggling backend. Use draining so that deployments do not drop requests. Set connection, request and timeout limits, and rate limits per client, to protect backends and to fail fast with a clear status.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"zones-and-regions\">Zones and regions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spread the tier across at least three zones, with enough spare capacity that losing one zone does not overload the rest. Use health based traffic steering at the DNS or anycast level for regional failover, and test it. Keep configuration the same in every location and deploy it in rings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-modes\">Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Overload needs load shedding at the edge. A bad configuration pushed everywhere can take the whole tier down, so validate it, roll it out in stages and keep the last good version. An expired certificate at the edge is a total outage, so automate renewal and alert early. Keep a documented way to bypass the tier in an emergency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cl&gt;l4;l4&gt;l7;l7&gt;be\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Design: an ingress and load balancing tier\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Clients\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"l4\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Layer 4 tier\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"l7\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Layer 7 proxies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"be\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backends\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Clients to Layer 4 tier; 2, Layer 4 tier to Layer 7 proxies; 3, Layer 7 proxies to Backends.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Clients \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> External traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Burst.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"l4\">\u003cdt>Layer 4 tier \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Spreads connections.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"l7\">\u003cdt>Layer 7 proxies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> TLS and routing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"be\">\u003cdt>Backends \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Health checked.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unhealthy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-typo-in-the-routing-configuration\">Incident: One typo in the routing configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small change to the proxy configuration made every route return 404 for several minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/staged/nginx.conf\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot; https://edge.example.com/api/health\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/staged/nginx.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/staged/nginx.conf test is successful\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot;\u003c/span> https://edge.example.com/api/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">404\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The syntax check passed, but a variable in a routing rule was empty, so no route matched. The change had been pushed to all proxies at once. The team rolled back to the last good file, then added a staged rollout of one proxy first, a synthetic request check against real routes before widening and automatic rollback if the check failed. The edge configuration became a reviewed and tested artifact.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Health checks that only test an open port\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pushing configuration to all proxies at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity with no room to lose a zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No limits on connections and rates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Manual certificate renewal at the edge\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"layer-4-and-layer-7","text":"Layer 4 and layer 7"},{"id":"health-and-limits","text":"Health and limits"},{"id":"zones-and-regions","text":"Zones and regions"},{"id":"failure-modes","text":"Failure modes"},{"id":"incident-one-typo-in-the-routing-configuration","text":"Incident: One typo in the routing configuration"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Envoy documentation","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"},{"t":"Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"},{"t":"NGINX load balancing","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/load_balancing.html"}],"text":"Every request passes through the front door. It terminates TLS, spreads load and shields backends, and when it fails everything behind it looks down. What you will learn Requirements: traffic, latency, protocols and availability Layer 4 or layer 7 and where to terminate TLS Health checks, draining and connection limits Zones, regions and failover Failure modes: overload, bad configuration and certificate expiry Requirements Estimate requests per second at peak, concurrent connections, bandwidth and the protocols in use, such as HTTP, gRPC and WebSocket. Set an availability goal for the tier, which must be higher than for the services behind it. Ask whether clients are external, internal or both, since that changes the security design. Layer 4 and layer 7 A layer 4 balancer forwards connections and is fast and simple. A layer 7 proxy understands HTTP, so it can route by host and path, retry, limit rates and add headers, at a higher cost per request. Many designs use a layer 4 tier in front of a layer 7 tier. Terminate TLS at the layer 7 tier, and re encrypt to the backends if the network is not trusted. Health and limits Health checks should test that the backend can do useful work, not only that the port is open, but they must be cheap and must not take down a struggling backend. Use draining so that deployments do not drop requests. Set connection, request and timeout limits, and rate limits per client, to protect backends and to fail fast with a clear status. Zones and regions Spread the tier across at least three zones, with enough spare capacity that losing one zone does not overload the rest. Use health based traffic steering at the DNS or anycast level for regional failover, and test it. Keep configuration the same in every location and deploy it in rings. Failure modes Overload needs load shedding at the edge. A bad configuration pushed everywhere can take the whole tier down, so validate it, roll it out in stages and keep the last good version. An expired certificate at the edge is a total outage, so automate renewal and alert early. Keep a documented way to bypass the tier in an emergency. Design: an ingress and load balancing tier Flow in order: 1, Clients to Layer 4 tier; 2, Layer 4 tier to Layer 7 proxies; 3, Layer 7 proxies to Backends. Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Clients Outside party What it does: External traffic. If it fails or is weak: Burst. Layer 4 tier Service What it does: Spreads connections. If it fails or is weak: Overload. Layer 7 proxies Service What it does: TLS and routing. If it fails or is weak: Bad config. Backends Service What it does: Health checked. If it fails or is weak: Unhealthy. Incident: One typo in the routing configuration Incident A small change to the proxy configuration made every route return 404 for several minutes. $ nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/staged/nginx.conf nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/staged/nginx.conf test is successful $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" https://edge.example.com/api/health 404 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The syntax check passed, but a variable in a routing rule was empty, so no route matched. The change had been pushed to all proxies at once. The team rolled back to the last good file, then added a staged rollout of one proxy first, a synthetic request check against real routes before widening and automatic rollback if the check failed. The edge configuration became a reviewed and tested artifact. Common mistakes Health checks that only test an open port Pushing configuration to all proxies at once Capacity with no room to lose a zone No limits on connections and rates Manual certificate renewal at the edge"},"infra-design/design-a-backup-and-disaster-recovery-platform":{"html":"\u003cp>A backup is only proven by a restore. The design starts from how much loss and downtime the business accepts, and ends with regular tests that show it can be met.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Recovery objectives per service tier\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backup methods: snapshots, logical and continuous\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting backups: immutability, separation and access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated restore tests and measuring recovery time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure modes: silent failure, deleted backups and slow restores\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objectives\">Objectives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each service tier agree a recovery point objective, how much data may be lost, and a recovery time objective, how long it may be down. A payments ledger may need near zero loss and minutes of downtime, while an internal wiki may accept a day. Objectives decide the method and the cost, and must be agreed with the business owners.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"methods\">Methods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Snapshots are fast and suit volumes and cloud databases. Logical backups, such as dumps, are portable and useful for partial restores but slow at scale. Continuous archiving of the transaction log allows recovery to a point in time. Combine them by tier, and remember that application consistency needs a quiesce or a database aware method.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protection\">Protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backups are a target for attackers. Keep copies in a separate account or provider with different credentials, make them immutable for a set period, require approval to delete and encrypt them with keys that are also backed up elsewhere. A backup that an administrator of the production environment can delete is not safe from ransomware.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore-tests\">Restore tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automate restores into an isolated environment on a schedule, verify the data with checks such as row counts and application start up, and measure the time taken. Report results per service. Sample large estates so that every service is tested at least once in a period. A failing test is an incident for the backup system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-modes\">Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backups can fail silently, so alert on missing runs and on size changes. Retention clean up can delete too much, so protect it with limits. Restore time can exceed the objective as data grows, so track it. Plan how the backup platform itself is restored, including the credentials and keys it needs, which must not depend on the thing that is lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"tier&gt;bk;bk&gt;vault;vault&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Design: a backup and disaster recovery platform\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tier\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tiers and objectives\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bk\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backup jobs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vault\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Isolated copies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Tiers and objectives to Backup jobs; 2, Backup jobs to Isolated copies; 3, Isolated copies to Restore tests.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tier\">\u003cdt>Tiers and objectives \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RPO and RTO.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unagreed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bk\">\u003cdt>Backup jobs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Method per tier.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silent fail.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vault\">\u003cdt>Isolated copies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Immutable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deletable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Restore tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Automated.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never run.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backups-that-could-not-be-restored\">Incident: The backups that could not be restored\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a storage failure, the team found that the newest backups were empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 ls s3://backups-prod/db/ --human-readable | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://backups-prod/db/ --human-readable | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-11-08 02:00:11    0 Bytes db-20261108.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-11-09 02:00:09    0 Bytes db-20261109.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-11-10 02:00:12    0 Bytes db-20261110.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A credential change had made the dump command fail, but the job still reported success because the script ignored the exit status. Three days of backups were empty, and the restore used an older copy, losing three days of data. The team fixed the script, added size and age checks, started weekly automated restore tests with data verification and made missing or tiny backups page the owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never testing restores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups deletable from production credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring exit codes in backup scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on backup size or age\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore plan that depends on the lost system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"objectives","text":"Objectives"},{"id":"methods","text":"Methods"},{"id":"protection","text":"Protection"},{"id":"restore-tests","text":"Restore tests"},{"id":"failure-modes","text":"Failure modes"},{"id":"incident-the-backups-that-could-not-be-restored","text":"Incident: The backups that could not be restored"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Backup","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html"},{"t":"Velero","u":"https://velero.io/docs/"},{"t":"pgBackRest","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/"}],"text":"A backup is only proven by a restore. The design starts from how much loss and downtime the business accepts, and ends with regular tests that show it can be met. What you will learn Recovery objectives per service tier Backup methods: snapshots, logical and continuous Protecting backups: immutability, separation and access Automated restore tests and measuring recovery time Failure modes: silent failure, deleted backups and slow restores Objectives For each service tier agree a recovery point objective, how much data may be lost, and a recovery time objective, how long it may be down. A payments ledger may need near zero loss and minutes of downtime, while an internal wiki may accept a day. Objectives decide the method and the cost, and must be agreed with the business owners. Methods Snapshots are fast and suit volumes and cloud databases. Logical backups, such as dumps, are portable and useful for partial restores but slow at scale. Continuous archiving of the transaction log allows recovery to a point in time. Combine them by tier, and remember that application consistency needs a quiesce or a database aware method. Protection Backups are a target for attackers. Keep copies in a separate account or provider with different credentials, make them immutable for a set period, require approval to delete and encrypt them with keys that are also backed up elsewhere. A backup that an administrator of the production environment can delete is not safe from ransomware. Restore tests Automate restores into an isolated environment on a schedule, verify the data with checks such as row counts and application start up, and measure the time taken. Report results per service. Sample large estates so that every service is tested at least once in a period. A failing test is an incident for the backup system. Failure modes Backups can fail silently, so alert on missing runs and on size changes. Retention clean up can delete too much, so protect it with limits. Restore time can exceed the objective as data grows, so track it. Plan how the backup platform itself is restored, including the credentials and keys it needs, which must not depend on the thing that is lost. Design: a backup and disaster recovery platform Flow in order: 1, Tiers and objectives to Backup jobs; 2, Backup jobs to Isolated copies; 3, Isolated copies to Restore tests. Step Service Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Tiers and objectives Step What it does: RPO and RTO. If it fails or is weak: Unagreed. Backup jobs Service What it does: Method per tier. If it fails or is weak: Silent fail. Isolated copies Security What it does: Immutable. If it fails or is weak: Deletable. Restore tests Observability What it does: Automated. If it fails or is weak: Never run. Incident: The backups that could not be restored Incident After a storage failure, the team found that the newest backups were empty. $ aws s3 ls s3://backups-prod/db/ --human-readable | tail -3 2026-11-08 02:00:11 0 Bytes db-20261108.dump 2026-11-09 02:00:09 0 Bytes db-20261109.dump 2026-11-10 02:00:12 0 Bytes db-20261110.dump (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A credential change had made the dump command fail, but the job still reported success because the script ignored the exit status. Three days of backups were empty, and the restore used an older copy, losing three days of data. The team fixed the script, added size and age checks, started weekly automated restore tests with data verification and made missing or tiny backups page the owner. Common mistakes Never testing restores Backups deletable from production credentials Ignoring exit codes in backup scripts No alert on backup size or age Restore plan that depends on the lost system"}});